    "# Linear Regression: Cost Function\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1. Linear Regression Model\n",
    "\n",
    "Linear regression is a supervised learning algorithm that models the relationship between an input variable `x` and a target value `y`.\n",
    "\n",
    "The model is represented by:\n",
    "\n",
    "f_w,b(x) = w * x + b\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `x` is the input feature.\n",
    "- `f_w,b(x)` is the model's prediction.\n",
    "- `w` is the weight, which controls the slope of the line.\n",
    "- `b` is the bias, or intercept, which determines where the line crosses the y-axis.\n",
    "\n",
    "Each pair of values `(w, b)` defines a different line.\n",
    "\n",
    "The objective of training is to find values of `w` and `b` that make the predictions as close as possible to the actual training data.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2. Training Data and Predictions\n",
    "\n",
    "A training dataset consists of multiple training examples.\n",
    "\n",
    "Each example contains:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `x^(i)` — the input value.\n",
    "- `y^(i)` — the actual target value.\n",
    "\n",
    "For each training example, the model produces a prediction:\n",
    "\n",
    "ŷ^(i) = f_w,b(x^(i))\n",
    "\n",
    "The difference between the prediction and the actual target is called the error or residual.\n",
    "\n",
    "A good model produces predictions with small errors across the training dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 3. Cost Function\n",
    "\n",
    "The cost function measures how well the linear regression model fits the training data.\n",
    "\n",
    "For linear regression, the squared error cost function is:\n",
    "\n",
    "J(w,b) = (1 / 2m) * Σ(f_w,b(x^(i)) - y^(i))²\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `J(w,b)` is the cost.\n",
    "- `m` is the number of training examples.\n",
    "- `f_w,b(x^(i))` is the prediction for example `i`.\n",
    "- `y^(i)` is the actual target.\n",
    "- `(prediction - target)²` is the squared error.\n",
    "\n",
    "The factor `1 / 2m` is used mainly for mathematical convenience when deriving the optimization algorithm.\n",
    "\n",
    "The important idea is:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Higher cost → the model fits the data poorly.\n",
    "- Lower cost → the model fits the data better.\n",
    "- Minimum cost → the parameters produce the best fit according to the cost function.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 4. Why Squared Error?\n",
    "\n",
    "The cost function uses the squared difference between the prediction and the actual target.\n",
    "\n",
    "If the error is:\n",
    "\n",
    "prediction - target\n",
    "\n",
    "then the squared error is:\n",
    "\n",
    "(prediction - target)²\n",
    "\n",
    "Squaring the error has two important effects:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Positive and negative errors do not cancel each other out.\n",
    "2. Larger errors receive greater penalty.\n",
    "\n",
    "The cost function then averages these squared errors across all training examples.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 5. Simplified Linear Regression\n",
    "\n",
    "To make the concept easier to understand, the model can initially be simplified by setting:\n",
    "\n",
    "b = 0\n",
    "\n",
    "The model becomes:\n",
    "\n",
    "f_w(x) = w * x\n",
    "\n",
    "There is now only one parameter: `w`.\n",
    "\n",
    "The cost function therefore depends only on `w`:\n",
    "\n",
    "J(w)\n",
    "\n",
    "Different values of `w` produce different lines and therefore different cost values.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `w = 0` produces a horizontal line.\n",
    "- `w = 0.5` produces a line with a positive slope.\n",
    "- `w = 1` may produce a perfect fit for a particular dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "If `w = 1` perfectly matches the training data, the prediction errors are zero and the cost is also zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 6. Visualizing the Cost Function with One Parameter\n",
    "\n",
    "When only `w` is being considered, the cost function can be plotted as:\n",
    "\n",
    "J(w) versus w\n",
    "\n",
    "For linear regression, this produces a U-shaped curve.\n",
    "\n",
    "Each point on the curve represents the cost produced by a particular value of `w`.\n",
    "\n",
    "The bottom of the curve represents the minimum cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Moving toward the bottom → better model parameters.\n",
    "- Moving away from the bottom → higher cost.\n",
    "- The lowest point → parameter value that minimizes the cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "This visualization helps connect the model parameters with the resulting model performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 7. Two Parameters: w and b\n",
    "\n",
    "The complete linear regression model contains two parameters:\n",
    "\n",
    "f_w,b(x) = w * x + b\n",
    "\n",
    "The parameters have different roles:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `w` controls the slope.\n",
    "- `b` controls the vertical position of the line.\n",
    "\n",
    "Changing either parameter changes the model's predictions and therefore changes the cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "The goal is to find the combination of `w` and `b` that minimizes:\n",
    "\n",
    "J(w,b)\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 8. Visualizing the Cost Function in 3D\n",
    "\n",
    "With two parameters, `w` and `b`, the cost function has two input dimensions.\n",
    "\n",
    "The cost function can therefore be visualized as a 3D surface.\n",
    "\n",
    "Each point on the surface represents:\n",
    "\n",
    "(w, b, J(w,b))\n",
    "\n",
    "The horizontal dimensions represent different values of `w` and `b`, while the vertical dimension represents the resulting cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "For linear regression, the surface has a bowl-like shape.\n",
    "\n",
    "The bottom of the bowl represents the minimum cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "This means the corresponding `(w,b)` pair provides the best fit to the training data according to the cost function.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9. Contour Plot\n",
    "\n",
    "A contour plot provides a 2D view of the same cost function.\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of displaying height directly, it uses curves to represent locations with the same cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "Each contour represents a constant cost value.\n",
    "\n",
    "The contours often appear as concentric ellipses.\n",
    "\n",
    "The center of the smallest ellipse corresponds to the minimum cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "This can be understood similarly to a topographical map:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Outer contours → higher cost.\n",
    "- Inner contours → lower cost.\n",
    "- Center → minimum cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "Contour plots make it easier to visualize how changing `w` and `b` affects the cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 10. Parameter Choices and Model Fit\n",
    "\n",
    "Every pair `(w,b)` creates a different line:\n",
    "\n",
    "f(x) = w * x + b\n",
    "\n",
    "Some parameter combinations produce poor fits.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `w = -0.15`, `b = 800` produces a line with a high cost.\n",
    "- `w = 0`, `b = 360` produces a horizontal line and may have a lower cost than the previous example, but it can still be a poor fit.\n",
    "- Other parameter combinations can produce even larger errors.\n",
    "- Parameters close to the minimum of the cost function produce a line that fits the training data much better.\n",
    "\n",
    "The important relationship is:\n",
    "\n",
    "Parameter choice → Model line → Predictions → Errors → Cost\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 11. Understanding the Optimization Problem\n",
    "\n",
    "Training linear regression can be viewed as an optimization problem.\n",
    "\n",
    "The objective is:\n",
    "\n",
    "Find `w` and `b` that minimize `J(w,b)`.\n",
    "\n",
    "In other words, we want to search through possible parameter values until we find parameters that produce the lowest possible cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "Manually trying different values of `w` and `b` is inefficient.\n",
    "\n",
    "Machine learning therefore uses optimization algorithms to automatically search for good parameter values.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 12. Gradient Descent\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradient descent is an optimization algorithm used to minimize the cost function.\n",
    "\n",
    "For linear regression, gradient descent adjusts `w` and `b` iteratively to move toward the minimum of the cost function.\n",
    "\n",
    "The basic idea is:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Start with initial values for `w` and `b`.\n",
    "2. Calculate the cost.\n",
    "3. Determine the direction in which the cost decreases.\n",
    "4. Update `w` and `b`.\n",
    "5. Repeat the process.\n",
    "\n",
    "The parameters gradually move toward values that minimize the cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradient descent is not limited to linear regression. It is a fundamental optimization algorithm used in many machine learning models.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 13. Overall Concept\n",
    "\n",
    "The complete relationship can be summarized as:\n",
    "\n",
    "Training Data\n",
    "→ Choose parameters `w` and `b`\n",
    "→ Build the linear model\n",
    "→ Generate predictions\n",
    "→ Calculate errors\n",
    "→ Calculate cost\n",
    "→ Adjust parameters\n",
    "→ Reduce cost\n",
    "→ Repeat\n",
    "\n",
    "The key idea is that the cost function provides a numerical measure of how well the model fits the training data.\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradient descent can then use this cost function to automatically find parameter values that minimize the cost."
   ]
